(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "9.4", title: "Inside the CPU", q: "How does a processor actually run a program?", lessons: [
{ t: "Von Neumann and the stored-program concept", o: "I can describe the Von Neumann architecture and the stored-program concept.",
  k: ["Von Neumann architecture: a design in which instructions and data share the same memory", "stored-program concept: the program is held in memory and its instructions are fetched one at a time", "main memory: the RAM that holds programs and data in use"],
  d: ["Early computers were reprogrammed by rewiring them. What problem does that cause?", "Changing the program is slow and difficult; the machine is stuck doing one job."],
  s: ["Before stored programs|Programs were set with switches and cables|Changing the job meant rebuilding the machine|In 1945 John von Neumann described a better design",
      "The stored-program concept|The program is stored in memory, like data|The CPU fetches each instruction and carries it out|Load a different program to do a different job",
      "The Von Neumann architecture|A CPU, main memory and input/output devices|Instructions and data share the same memory|Buses connect them"],
  a: "Label the Von Neumann architecture diagram, then explain in a paragraph why storing the program in memory made computers general-purpose. Support: use the label cards. Stretch: research one disadvantage of instructions and data sharing the same memory and bus.",
  w: [["What is the stored-program concept?", "A program is held in memory and its instructions are fetched and run one at a time."], ["In Von Neumann architecture, where are instructions and data stored?", "Together in the same main memory."], ["Name the three main parts of the Von Neumann architecture.", "The CPU, main memory, and input/output devices (connected by buses)."], ["Why is a stored-program computer called general-purpose?", "It can do a different job just by loading a different program."], ["Explain one advantage of storing instructions in memory instead of setting them with wires.", "Programs can be changed or replaced quickly without changing the hardware."]],
  x: ["Who described the architecture used by most computers today?", "John von Neumann."] },
{ t: "Components of the CPU", o: "I can state the job of the control unit, the ALU and the registers.",
  k: ["control unit (CU): directs the fetch-decode-execute cycle and sends control signals", "arithmetic logic unit (ALU): carries out calculations and logical comparisons", "register: a tiny, very fast store inside the CPU"],
  d: ["A restaurant kitchen has a head chef, cooks and small trays beside each cook. Which part of a CPU might each be like?", "Head chef: control unit. Cooks: ALU. Trays: registers."],
  s: ["Control unit|Fetches instructions and decodes them|Sends signals to make other parts act|Controls the timing using the clock",
      "ALU|Arithmetic: add, subtract|Logic: AND, OR, NOT, compare|Results go to the accumulator",
      "Registers|PC: address of the next instruction|MAR: address being read or written|MDR: data just read or about to be written|CIR: instruction being run|ACC: result of calculations"],
  a: "Match each component and register to its job, then label the CPU diagram. Write a sentence for each register using the word address or data correctly. Support: use the matching cards. Stretch: explain why registers make the CPU faster than if it used only main memory.",
  w: [["What does the ALU do?", "It carries out calculations and logical comparisons."], ["What does the control unit do?", "It directs the fetch-decode-execute cycle and sends control signals."], ["What does the program counter (PC) hold?", "The address of the next instruction to be fetched."], ["Which register holds the instruction currently being run?", "The current instruction register (CIR)."], ["Explain the difference between the MAR and the MDR.", "The MAR holds an address in memory. The MDR holds the data or instruction read from, or to be written to, that address."]],
  x: ["Which register holds the result of a calculation?", "The accumulator (ACC)."] },
{ t: "Lab: human CPU", o: "I can act out how the parts of the CPU work together to run a program.",
  k: ["instruction: one operation, such as LOAD or ADD", "opcode: the part of an instruction that says what to do", "operand: the part of an instruction that says what to do it to"],
  d: ["In the instruction ADD 7, which part is the opcode and which is the operand?", "ADD is the opcode; 7 is the operand."],
  s: ["Roles|Memory: a grid of envelopes, each with an address|PC, MAR, MDR, CIR, ACC: students with mini-whiteboards|Control unit: calls each step|ALU: does the arithmetic",
      "The program|Three instructions in memory: load, add, store|Data in other envelopes|The control unit says 'fetch', 'decode', 'execute'",
      "Watch for|Which registers change at each step|What travels from memory and what travels to it|How the PC moves on"],
  a: "Act out the three-instruction program twice, swapping roles, while the rest of the class records every register change in a table. Then run the same program in the CPU and assembly page of CS Toolkit and compare. Support: be an observer with the record sheet first. Stretch: write a four-instruction program for another group to act out.",
  w: [["What is an opcode?", "The part of an instruction that says what to do."], ["What is an operand?", "The part of an instruction that says what to do it to: a value or an address."], ["In the human CPU, which role held the address of the next instruction?", "The program counter (PC)."], ["What did the ALU student do?", "The arithmetic, such as adding two numbers."], ["Explain one way the human model is different from a real CPU.", "For example a real CPU is billions of times faster, uses electrical signals, or does several steps at once."]],
  x: ["Which register changed most often?", "For example the MAR or the PC, from the student's record."] },
{ t: "Buses: address, data and control", o: "I can describe the three buses and what each carries.",
  k: ["bus: a set of parallel wires that carries signals between components", "address bus: carries memory addresses from the CPU to memory, in one direction", "data bus: carries data and instructions between the CPU and memory, in both directions"],
  d: ["A letter needs an address and a message. Which bus do you think carries each?", "The address bus carries the address; the data bus carries the message."],
  s: ["Three buses|Address bus: CPU to memory only|Data bus: both directions|Control bus: signals such as read, write and the clock",
      "Reading from memory|The address goes along the address bus|The control unit sends a read signal on the control bus|The data comes back along the data bus",
      "Width matters|A wider data bus carries more bits at once|A wider address bus can reach more memory locations|16 address lines can reach 2 to the power 16, which is 65 536, addresses"],
  a: "Complete the bus table (what each carries, direction, example), then annotate diagrams of a memory read and a memory write. Support: use the arrow cards to show directions. Stretch: calculate how many addresses 8, 16 and 32 address lines can reach.",
  w: [["What is a bus?", "A set of parallel wires that carries signals between components."], ["Which bus carries addresses, and in which direction?", "The address bus, from the CPU to memory only."], ["Why is the data bus two-way?", "Data is read from memory into the CPU and written from the CPU into memory."], ["What does the control bus carry?", "Control signals such as read, write and clock signals."], ["How many locations can a 16-line address bus reach?", "65 536."]],
  x: ["Which bus is one-way?", "The address bus."] },
{ t: "The fetch stage, register by register", o: "I can describe the fetch stage of the fetch-decode-execute cycle.",
  k: ["fetch-decode-execute cycle: the cycle the CPU repeats to run every instruction", "fetch: bringing the next instruction from memory into the CPU", "register transfer: copying a value from one register or memory location to another"],
  d: ["The PC holds 20. Which memory address will the next instruction come from?", "20."],
  s: ["Fetch, step by step|The address in the PC is copied to the MAR|The instruction at that address is copied into the MDR|The PC is incremented to point to the next instruction|The instruction is copied from the MDR to the CIR",
      "In register transfer notation|MAR ← [PC]|MDR ← [[MAR]] and PC ← [PC] + 1|CIR ← [MDR]",
      "Reading the notation|[PC] means 'the contents of the PC'|[[MAR]] means 'the contents of the address held in the MAR'|← means 'is copied to'"],
  a: "Put the fetch cards in order, then write each step in words and in register transfer notation. Complete the register table for fetching the instruction at address 20. Support: use the step cards with pictures. Stretch: explain why the PC is incremented during the fetch and not after the instruction has run.",
  w: [["What is the first step of the fetch stage?", "The address in the PC is copied to the MAR."], ["Where does the instruction go when it arrives from memory?", "The MDR."], ["What happens to the PC during the fetch stage?", "It is incremented to point to the next instruction."], ["Write CIR ← [MDR] in words.", "The contents of the MDR are copied to the CIR."], ["The PC holds 20 at the start of a fetch. What does it hold at the end, and what does the MAR hold?", "The PC holds 21 and the MAR holds 20."]],
  x: ["Name the four registers used in the fetch stage.", "PC, MAR, MDR, CIR."] },
{ t: "Lab: step through fetch in the simulator", o: "I can watch and record every register transfer of the fetch stage.",
  k: ["micro-step: one register transfer within the cycle", "simulator: a program that imitates a real system", "observe: to watch carefully and record"],
  d: ["Predict: after the first micro-step of a fetch, which register has changed?", "The MAR."],
  s: ["Open the simulator|CPU and assembly in CS Toolkit|Load 'Add two numbers'|Press Step: one register transfer at a time",
      "Record|Write the transfer shown, such as MAR ← [PC]|Write the new value of the register that changed (it is highlighted)|Notice which buses light up",
      "Questions to answer|When is the data bus used?|Why does the MAR change again during execute?|What is the PC after three instructions?"],
  a: "Step through the first two instructions of 'Add two numbers', recording every micro-step in the table. Answer the three questions. Support: record the first instruction only. Stretch: step through 'Indirect addressing' and explain why the MAR changes twice in the execute stage.",
  w: [["What does the simulator show for the first micro-step?", "MAR ← [PC]."], ["Which register changes at the step MDR ← [[MAR]]?", "The MDR."], ["When the first instruction has been fetched, what does the PC hold if the program starts at 0?", "1."], ["When is the data bus used during the fetch stage?", "When the instruction travels from memory to the MDR."], ["Explain why the MAR changes during the execute stage of LDD NUM1.", "LDD must read data from memory, so the data's address is placed in the MAR."]],
  x: ["How many micro-steps does the simulator use for the fetch stage?", "Four."] },
{ t: "Decode and execute", o: "I can describe the decode and execute stages.",
  k: ["decode: the control unit works out what the instruction means", "execute: the instruction is carried out", "accumulator: the register that holds results from the ALU"],
  d: ["The CIR holds LDD 30. What do you think the CPU will do?", "Load the value stored at address 30 into the accumulator."],
  s: ["Decode|The control unit splits the instruction into opcode and operand|It works out which parts of the CPU are needed|LDD 30: opcode LDD, operand 30",
      "Execute|The CU sends signals to carry out the instruction|LDD 30: MAR ← 30, MDR ← [30], ACC ← [MDR]|ADD 31: the ALU adds the value at 31 to the ACC",
      "Then repeat|The cycle starts again with the next fetch|Millions or billions of times a second|Until the program ends"],
  a: "For six instructions, write what happens in the decode and execute stages and which registers change. Check two in the simulator. Support: complete three using the description cards. Stretch: describe the execute stage of STO, which writes to memory instead of reading.",
  w: [["What happens in the decode stage?", "The control unit works out what the instruction means: its opcode and operand."], ["Which part of the CPU does the addition in ADD?", "The ALU."], ["Where is the result of ADD stored?", "In the accumulator."], ["Which registers change when LDD 30 is executed?", "The MAR, the MDR and the ACC."], ["Explain the difference between executing LDD and executing STO.", "LDD reads a value from memory into the ACC. STO writes the value in the ACC to memory."]],
  x: ["Name the three stages of the cycle in order.", "Fetch, decode, execute."] },
{ t: "Tracing a whole program", o: "I can trace the accumulator and memory through a short program.",
  k: ["trace: to record the values of registers and memory as a program runs", "immediate: the operand is the value itself, written with #", "direct: the operand is the address of the value"],
  d: ["What is the difference between LDM #5 and LDD 5?", "LDM #5 loads the number 5. LDD 5 loads the value stored at address 5."],
  s: ["Instructions used|LDM #n: load the number n into the ACC|LDD addr: load the value at addr|ADD and SUB: add or subtract (with # for a number)|STO addr: store the ACC at addr|END: stop",
      "Example|>> LDM #5|>> ADD #3|>> STO X|>> LDD X|>> SUB #2|>> END",
      "Trace it|ACC after each instruction: 5, 8, 8, 8, 6|X holds 8 at the end|One row per instruction"],
  a: "Trace the four programs on the sheet, recording the ACC and any memory that changes after each instruction. Support: trace the first two with the instruction card. Stretch: write a program that calculates 3 x 4 using only LDM, ADD and STO.",
  w: [["What does LDM #7 do?", "Loads the number 7 into the accumulator."], ["Give the ACC after each instruction of the example.", "5, 8, 8, 8, 6."], ["What value is stored at X at the end of the example?", "8."], ["NUM1 holds 12 and NUM2 holds 30. Trace LDD NUM1, ADD NUM2, STO TOTAL. Give the ACC each time and TOTAL at the end.", "ACC: 12, 42, 42. TOTAL: 42."], ["Explain why STO does not change the accumulator.", "It copies the ACC's value into memory; the ACC itself is only read."]],
  x: ["Which addressing mode uses the # symbol?", "Immediate."] },
{ t: "Lab: predict, then check in the simulator", o: "I can predict register values and check them in the simulator.",
  k: ["prediction: what you expect to happen", "verify: to check that a result is correct", "discrepancy: a difference between two results"],
  d: ["Predict the ACC after LDM #10 then SUB #4.", "6."],
  s: ["Method|Trace the program on paper first|Type it into the CPU and assembly page|Use 'Run one instruction' and compare after each",
      "Look closely|The memory table highlights what was read or written|The ACC shows its value in binary as well|Labels such as NUM1 stand for addresses",
      "When you disagree|Find the first instruction where the values differ|Was it # versus an address?|Was STO mixed up with LDD?"],
  a: "Trace and then check the five programs, recording any discrepancies and their causes. Support: check the first three. Stretch: write a program for a partner with one deliberate bug, and find theirs.",
  w: [["What does 'Run one instruction' do in the simulator?", "Runs a complete fetch-decode-execute cycle for one instruction."], ["Trace: LDM #10, SUB #4, STO A, ADD A. Give the final ACC.", "12."], ["A student traced LDD 5 as loading the number 5. What was the mistake?", "LDD 5 loads the value stored at address 5, not the number 5."], ["Why do labels make assembly programs easier to read?", "Names such as TOTAL say what is stored, instead of a bare address number."], ["Explain how the simulator helped you find a discrepancy.", "It shows each register after every instruction, so the first difference from my trace is easy to spot."]],
  x: ["What was your most common tracing mistake?", "A named mistake."] },
{ t: "Assembly language: load, add, store", o: "I can write simple assembly language programs.",
  k: ["assembly language: a low-level language where each instruction matches one machine-code instruction", "mnemonic: a short code for an instruction, such as LDD or ADD", "assembler: software that translates assembly language into machine code"],
  d: ["Why do people write in Python instead of in binary machine code?", "Python is much easier to read, write and fix."],
  s: ["Levels of language|Machine code: binary the CPU runs directly|Assembly: mnemonics, one per machine instruction|High-level, such as Python: one line can become many instructions",
      "The 9618 instruction set|LDM, LDD, STO, ADD, SUB, INC, DEC|CMP, JMP, JPE, JPN, IN, OUT, END|You will meet the full set at A Level",
      "Writing a program|>> LDD NUM1|>> ADD NUM2|>> STO TOTAL|>> END|>> NUM1: 12|>> NUM2: 30|>> TOTAL: 0"],
  a: "Write assembly programs to add three numbers, subtract one number from another, and double a number (add it to itself). Run each in the simulator. Support: complete the three-number adder. Stretch: write a program that swaps the values in two memory locations.",
  w: [["What is assembly language?", "A low-level language where each instruction matches one machine-code instruction."], ["What is a mnemonic?", "A short code for an instruction, such as LDD."], ["What does an assembler do?", "It translates assembly language into machine code."], ["Write a program that doubles the value at N and stores it at D.", "LDD N\nADD N\nSTO D\nEND"], ["Give one reason to use assembly language instead of a high-level language.", "For example direct control of the hardware, or very fast and small code for embedded systems."]],
  x: ["Which instruction ends a program?", "END."] },
{ t: "Loops with compare and jump", o: "I can use compare and jump instructions to make a loop.",
  k: ["CMP: compares the accumulator with a value or the contents of an address", "jump: changes the PC so execution continues at a different instruction", "conditional jump: a jump that happens only if the last comparison was true (JPE) or false (JPN)"],
  d: ["In Python, a while loop goes back to its condition. How might a CPU go back to an earlier instruction?", "By changing the program counter to that instruction's address."],
  s: ["Jumps|JMP LOOP: always jump to the label LOOP|JPE DONE: jump if the last compare was equal|JPN LOOP: jump if it was not equal",
      "A countdown|>> LOOP: LDD COUNT|>>       CMP #0|>>       JPE DONE|>>       DEC ACC|>>       STO COUNT|>>       JMP LOOP|>> DONE:  END",
      "How a jump works|It simply puts a new address in the PC|The next fetch comes from there|This is how every loop and IF is built"],
  a: "Trace the countdown with COUNT starting at 3, recording the ACC and COUNT each time round. Then run it in the simulator with Play. Support: trace the first two passes. Stretch: change it to count up from 0 to 5.",
  w: [["What does CMP do?", "Compares the accumulator with a value or the contents of an address."], ["What is the difference between JPE and JPN?", "JPE jumps if the last comparison was equal; JPN jumps if it was not equal."], ["How does a jump change what the CPU does next?", "It puts a new address in the PC, so the next instruction is fetched from there."], ["COUNT starts at 3. What values does COUNT take before the program ends?", "3, 2, 1, 0."], ["Explain how the countdown program stops.", "When COUNT reaches 0, CMP #0 is equal, so JPE DONE jumps to END."]],
  x: ["Which register does a jump instruction change?", "The program counter (PC)."] },
{ t: "Lab: write a countdown and an adder", o: "I can write, run and debug assembly programs with loops.",
  k: ["debug: to find and fix errors", "infinite loop: a loop that never ends", "label: a name given to an address in an assembly program"],
  d: ["A countdown never stops. Suggest a cause.", "The counter is not decreased, or not stored back, so the comparison never becomes equal."],
  s: ["Challenge 1|Count down from 5 to 0, outputting each number|OUT sends the character in the ACC to the screen|Hint: add 48 to turn the number 5 into the character '5'",
      "Challenge 2|Add the numbers from N down to 1 and store the total|For N = 4 the total should be 10|You need a loop, a total and a counter",
      "Debugging|Use Step to watch each instruction|Check that the counter is stored back after DEC|Check which way round JPE and JPN are"],
  a: "Complete both challenges, testing each in the simulator, and record one bug you found and fixed. Support: complete challenge 2 from the part-written program. Stretch: write a program that multiplies two numbers by repeated addition.",
  w: [["What is a label?", "A name given to an address in an assembly program."], ["Why must the counter be stored back after DEC ACC?", "DEC only changes the ACC. If the new value is not stored, the counter in memory never changes."], ["For N = 4, what total should challenge 2 give?", "10 (4 + 3 + 2 + 1)."], ["A loop uses JPE LOOP when it should use JPN LOOP. What happens?", "The loop ends after one pass when it should continue, or continues when it should stop."], ["Describe a bug you found and how you fixed it.", "A specific bug and fix."]],
  x: ["Did both your programs give the correct result?", "Yes, or which one did not and why."] },
{ t: "Performance: clock speed, cores and cache", o: "I can explain how clock speed, cores and cache affect performance.",
  k: ["clock speed: the number of cycles the CPU performs each second, measured in hertz", "core: a complete processing unit inside a CPU", "cache: a small amount of very fast memory inside or next to the CPU"],
  d: ["A 3 GHz processor carries out how many cycles each second?", "3 billion."],
  s: ["Clock speed|More cycles each second means more instructions|3.5 GHz is faster than 2.0 GHz, other things being equal|Higher speeds make more heat",
      "Cores|Each core can run its own instructions|Four cores can work on four tasks at once|Only helps if the software can split its work",
      "Cache|Holds data and instructions used often|Much faster to reach than RAM|Bigger cache means fewer slow trips to RAM"],
  a: "Rank the four fictional processors for three tasks (word processing, video editing, a game) and justify each ranking. Answer the 'will it be twice as fast?' questions. Support: compare only clock speed and cores. Stretch: explain why a phone processor may run more slowly on purpose.",
  w: [["What does clock speed measure?", "The number of cycles the CPU performs each second."], ["What is a core?", "A complete processing unit inside a CPU."], ["Why does a larger cache improve performance?", "Frequently used data is found in the fast cache, so the CPU waits less for slower RAM."], ["A program cannot split its work. Will an 8-core CPU run it much faster than a 4-core CPU at the same clock speed? Explain.", "No. The program can only use one core, so the extra cores do not help it."], ["Explain why doubling the number of cores does not double the speed.", "Not all work can be split, the cores must coordinate, and they share memory and other resources."]],
  x: ["Name the three factors that affect CPU performance.", "Clock speed, number of cores, cache size."] },
{ t: "Embedded systems", o: "I can describe embedded systems and give examples.",
  k: ["embedded system: a computer built into a larger device to perform one dedicated task", "microcontroller: a CPU, memory and input/output on one chip", "dedicated: designed for one job"],
  d: ["Last year you programmed a micro:bit. Was that an embedded system? Why?", "It is a microcontroller used as one; when built into a product to do one job, it is an embedded system."],
  s: ["What makes it embedded|Inside another device|Does one task, often without a screen or keyboard|Program usually stored in ROM or flash memory",
      "Examples|Washing machine, microwave, thermostat|Car engine management, airbag controller|Traffic lights, fitness tracker, smart speaker",
      "Compared with a PC|Small, cheap and low-power|Very reliable for its one job|Hard or impossible to upgrade or reprogram for another job"],
  a: "Sort 15 devices into embedded systems and general-purpose computers with reasons, then describe three embedded systems as inputs, process and outputs. Support: sort ten devices. Stretch: explain why embedded systems that connect to the internet bring new security risks.",
  w: [["What is an embedded system?", "A computer built into a larger device to perform one dedicated task."], ["Give three examples of embedded systems.", "Any three, for example a washing machine, thermostat, car engine controller, microwave."], ["Give two advantages of embedded systems over a general-purpose computer.", "Any two: smaller, cheaper, use less power, more reliable for their one task."], ["Give one disadvantage.", "They are hard to upgrade or reprogram for a different job."], ["Describe a washing machine's embedded system as inputs, process and outputs.", "Inputs: buttons, water-level and temperature sensors. Process: follows the chosen wash program. Outputs: motor, heater, valves, display."]],
  x: ["Where is an embedded system's program usually stored?", "In ROM or flash memory."] },
{ t: "Lab: choose a processor", o: "I can recommend a processor for a purpose and justify my choice.",
  k: ["specification: the list of a component's key features", "trade-off: gaining one thing by giving up another", "justify: to give reasons linked to the requirement"],
  d: ["A laptop must run all day on battery. Which matters more: highest clock speed or low power use?", "Low power use."],
  s: ["The processors|Five fictional specifications|Clock speed, cores, cache, power use, price|Read every line",
      "The scenarios|A school laptop that must last a full day|A video-editing workstation|A smart doorbell",
      "Recommend|Choose one processor for each scenario|Give two reasons linked to the scenario|Name one trade-off you accepted"],
  a: "Write a recommendation for each of the three scenarios with two justified reasons and one trade-off. Support: use the comparison grid with the key features highlighted. Stretch: suggest a sixth processor that would suit the doorbell even better, and justify each feature.",
  w: [["Which processor did you choose for video editing, and why?", "A processor with many cores, high clock speed and large cache, because video editing can use all of them."], ["Which processor did you choose for the doorbell, and why?", "A low-power, low-cost processor, because the task is simple and it runs all the time."], ["What trade-off did you accept for the school laptop?", "For example lower clock speed in return for longer battery life."], ["Why is the most expensive processor not always the best choice?", "It may use more power or cost more than the task needs."], ["Explain why cache size matters for the workstation.", "Large files and repeated operations benefit from fast access to data in cache instead of RAM."]],
  x: ["Which feature mattered most for the laptop?", "Power use (battery life)."] },
{ t: "Revision: label and narrate the cycle", o: "I can describe the whole fetch-decode-execute cycle in detail.",
  k: ["narrate: to describe a process step by step", "sequence: the correct order", "precise: using exactly the right terms"],
  d: ["Without notes, list the five registers and their jobs.", "PC, MAR, MDR, CIR, ACC with their jobs."],
  s: ["Fetch|MAR ← [PC]: address bus|MDR ← [[MAR]]: data bus, read signal on the control bus|PC ← [PC] + 1|CIR ← [MDR]",
      "Decode and execute|The control unit decodes opcode and operand|Execute: for example the ALU adds, result in the ACC|Then the next fetch",
      "Six-mark technique|One point per step, in order|Name the register and the bus|Use 'contents of' and 'address of' precisely"],
  a: "Label the full cycle diagram, then narrate the cycle aloud to a partner, who ticks each step on the checklist. Write a six-mark answer and mark it against the scheme. Support: use the step cards in order. Stretch: narrate the cycle for an instruction that writes to memory.",
  w: [["Write the fetch stage in register transfer notation.", "MAR ← [PC]; MDR ← [[MAR]]; PC ← [PC] + 1; CIR ← [MDR]."], ["Which bus carries the address from the MAR to memory?", "The address bus."], ["What happens in the decode stage?", "The control unit works out the opcode and operand of the instruction in the CIR."], ["Which component carries out ADD?", "The ALU."], ["Describe how one instruction is fetched from memory.", "The PC's address is copied to the MAR and sent on the address bus; the control unit sends a read signal; the instruction returns on the data bus to the MDR; the PC is incremented; the instruction is copied to the CIR."]],
  x: ["Which part of the cycle do you most need to revise?", "A named stage or register."] },
{ t: "Assessed test", o: "I can show what I know about how a processor runs a program.",
  k: ["test conditions: working alone, in silence, without notes", "levels-marked: an answer marked as a whole for quality and accuracy", "trace: to record values as a program runs"],
  d: ["What makes a six-mark answer about the cycle score highly?", "Every step in the right order, with registers and buses named precisely."],
  s: ["The test|A labelled architecture diagram|A six-mark description of the cycle|A short trace and a performance comparison",
      "Gaining marks|Name the register and say 'address' or 'data' correctly|Trace one row per instruction|Link performance to the task",
      "Checking|Is every register in your answer used correctly?|Is the order right?|Does your trace end on END?"],
  a: "Complete the test under test conditions. Support: an unlabelled diagram is provided for the six-mark question. Stretch: attempt the extension question on indirect addressing.",
  w: [["State the purpose of the MAR.", "It holds the address of the memory location to be read from or written to."], ["State the purpose of the ALU.", "It carries out calculations and logical comparisons."], ["Trace: LDM #6, ADD #9, STO R, SUB #5. Give the ACC after each instruction and R at the end.", "ACC: 6, 15, 15, 10. R: 15."], ["Explain why a computer with a larger cache may run programs faster.", "More frequently used data and instructions are held in fast cache, so the CPU waits less for slower RAM."], ["Give two characteristics of an embedded system.", "Any two: built into a larger device, does one dedicated task, low cost, low power, program in ROM or flash."]],
  x: ["Which question were you least sure about?", "A question number."] },
{ t: "Feedback and reflection", o: "I can correct my answers and reflect on my learning.",
  k: ["correction: a rewritten answer that fixes a mistake", "misconception: a wrong idea that causes mistakes", "target: a specific thing to work on next"],
  d: ["A common mistake is saying the MAR holds data. What does it hold?", "An address."],
  s: ["Read your feedback|Which mistakes were about registers, buses or tracing?|Was your six-mark answer in order?|Ask about anything unclear",
      "Correct|Rewrite the six-mark answer|Retrace any wrong trace in the simulator|Fix every 'address' or 'data' mix-up",
      "Reflect|How does Python code end up as fetch-decode-execute cycles?|What can I explain now that I could not before?|What is my target?"],
  a: "Correct your test using the simulator to check traces, then complete the unit reflection. Support: your teacher has chosen two questions to correct. Stretch: explain how a Python loop is turned into compare and jump instructions.",
